# Skill & doc authoring discipline

> **Standard reference — include in every generated project.** Read by whoever
> writes or edits a skill, a sub-agent system prompt, or a named doc (usually
> `dev-maintainer`). Loaded on demand, not in the boot set.
>
> A skill exists to wrangle determinism out of a stochastic system. The root
> virtue is **predictability** — the agent taking the same *process* every run,
> not producing the same output. Every rule below serves it.
> (Distilled from Matt Pocock's `mattpocock/skills`, MIT.)

## Invocation: two kinds of skill, two kinds of cost

- **Model-invoked** — carries a `description` the runtime loads **every turn**, so
  the agent (and other skills) can fire it autonomously. Costs **context load**.
- **User-invoked** (`disable-model-invocation: true`) — only a human typing its
  name fires it. Zero context load, but costs **cognitive load**: the human is
  the index that must remember it exists.

Pick model-invocation only when the agent must reach the skill on its own.
When user-invoked skills multiply past memory, add one **router skill** that
names the others and when to reach for each — do not promote them all.

## Writing the description (model-invoked only)

The description does two jobs: state what the skill is, and list the **branches**
that trigger it. Every word rides in context every turn, so prune it harder than
the body:

- **Front-load the skill's leading word** — the description is where it does its
  invocation work.
- **One trigger per branch.** Synonyms that rename the same branch are
  duplication — collapse them.
- Cut identity that is already in the body; keep triggers.

## Information hierarchy & progressive disclosure

Rank every piece of material by how immediately the agent needs it:

1. **In-skill step** — an ordered action in the skill body. Each step ends on a
   **completion criterion**: *checkable* (the agent can tell done from not-done)
   and, where it matters, *exhaustive* ("every modified file accounted for", not
   "produce a change list"). A vague criterion invites premature completion.
2. **In-skill reference** — a rule or fact consulted on demand, inline.
3. **External reference** — pushed into a sibling file behind a **context
   pointer**, loaded only when the pointer fires. The pointer's *wording* decides
   whether the agent actually reaches it.

**Progressive disclosure** is the move down this ladder so the top stays legible:
inline what every run needs; push behind a pointer what only some branches reach.
**Co-locate**: keep a concept's definition, rules, and caveats under one heading
rather than scattered.

## Leading words

A **leading word** is a compact concept already living in the model's pretraining
that anchors a whole region of behaviour in one token (*tight* loop, *red*/green,
*fog of war*, *handle not payload*). It pays twice: in the body it anchors
execution (same behaviour every time the word appears); in the description it
anchors invocation. Hunt for passages that restate one idea three ways and
collapse them into a single strong word.

## Pruning (run on every edit, not just at birth)

- **Single source of truth** — one authoritative place per meaning; changing a
  behaviour must be a one-place edit.
- **Relevance** — does the line still bear on what the skill does?
- **The no-op test** — sentence by sentence: does this sentence change behaviour
  versus the model's default? A line the model already obeys ("be thorough") pays
  load to say nothing. Delete the whole sentence; don't trim words from it.
- **Positive phrasing beats negation** — "don't think of an elephant" names the
  elephant. State the target behaviour so the banned one is never spoken; keep a
  prohibition only as a hard guardrail you can't phrase positively, and pair it
  with what to do instead.

## When to split a skill

Each cut spends one of the two loads, so split only when the cut earns it:

- **By invocation** — a distinct leading word should trigger on its own, or
  another skill must reach it. (Pays context load for a new description.)
- **By sequence** — the steps still ahead tempt the agent to rush the one in
  front of it; hiding them behind a split restores focus.

## Failure modes (diagnose a misbehaving skill against these)

| Mode | Symptom | Cure |
|---|---|---|
| **Premature completion** | a step ends before it's genuinely done | sharpen the completion criterion first; split the sequence only if the rush persists |
| **Duplication** | same meaning in >1 place | collapse to one source of truth |
| **Sediment** | stale layers accumulate because adding feels safe | upgrade by replacement (constitution rule) — rewrite, never append eras |
| **Sprawl** | too long even though every line is live | disclose reference behind pointers; split by branch |
| **No-op** | lines the model obeys by default | delete, or replace a weak leading word with a stronger one |
| **Negation** | steering by prohibition | restate as the positive target behaviour |
